※ pl4g-test: compile=error
※ pl4g-test: expect-diag 4538 LANG_IO_WITHOUT_A_DEVICE

※ `@[impure]` says a function changes something of the program's -- a variable
※ at the top level, memory somebody else made -- and nothing more.  Input and
※ output are the other permission: a device handed over carries it, and `@[io]`
※ says it where none is.  So a function marked only `@[impure]` that does input
※ or output without a device is refused as one marked nothing is, and one that
※ does both says `@[impure, io]`.

let std := ⎕import("std")

let waited: mut u64 = 0u64

@[impure]
fn tidy(slot: i64, ring: u64) → i64:
    waited ← waited + 1u64
    std.io.Pending.wait(std.io.Pending(.slot ← slot, .ring ← ring))

@[startup, impure]
fn main(init: std.Init) → u6:
    ⎕narrow(tidy(0i64, init.io.error.ring), ⌜u6⌝) ?? 0u6
